---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout
  title="Pool heater not working? Causes and fixes | GK Swimming Pools"
  description="Pool heater won't turn on or won't heat? See the common causes, what you can safely check, and how we repair pool heaters in the San Fernando Valley."
  canonical="https://gkswimmingpools.com/blog/pool-heater-not-working/"
>

  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify({
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "BlogPosting",
    "headline": "Pool Heater Not Working? Common Causes and How We Fix Them",
    "description": "Pool heater won't turn on or won't heat? See the common causes, what you can safely check, and how we repair pool heaters in the San Fernando Valley.",
    "datePublished": "2026-10-20",
    "author": { "@type": "LocalBusiness", "name": "GK Swimming Pools", "telephone": "(818) 804-8204" },
    "publisher": { "@type": "LocalBusiness", "name": "GK Swimming Pools", "url": "https://gkswimmingpools.com" },
    "image": "https://gkswimmingpools.com/images/blog/pool-heater-repair-hero.webp",
    "mainEntityOfPage": { "@type": "WebPage", "@id": "https://gkswimmingpools.com/blog/pool-heater-not-working/" }
  })} />

  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify({
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "FAQPage",
    "mainEntity": [
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "Why won't my pool heater turn on?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "The most common reason is low water flow. A dirty filter, a full pump basket, or a closed valve keeps the heater's pressure switch from closing, and the heater won't fire as a safety measure. Other causes include the thermostat set too low, the gas supply turned off, or a failed igniter or control board. Check the filter and settings first, then call a professional."
        }
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "Why does my pool heater turn on but not heat the water?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "If a gas heater runs but the water stays cold, it is often short cycling because of a flow problem or a dirty burner. A heat pump that runs without warming the water is usually dealing with cold outside air. Most heat pumps lose efficiency when the air drops below about 50 degrees. A pool cover also makes a big difference, because most heat loss happens at the surface."
        }
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "Should I repair or replace my pool heater?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "Pool heaters are built to last, and a quality heater can run 15 years or more depending on the brand. Many heater problems come from a single part or a blockage, so even an older heater is often worth repairing. A cracked heat exchanger or heavy corrosion is different, because that repair can cost close to a new unit. We give you both numbers so you can decide."
        }
      }
    ]
  })} />

  <section class="blog-hero">
    <div class="blog-hero__bg">
      <img src="/images/blog/pool-heater-repair-hero.webp"
        alt="GK Swimming Pools technician checking the control panel on a Sta-Rite gas pool heater during a pool heater repair"
        fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" width="1200" height="800" />
    </div>
    <div class="blog-hero__overlay"></div>
    <div class="container blog-hero__inner">
      <span class="eyebrow">Pool Repair Guide</span>
      <h1>Pool Heater Not Working? Common Causes and How We Fix Them</h1>
      <div class="blog-meta">
        <span>GK Swimming Pools</span>
        <span class="blog-meta__dot"></span>
        <span>5 min read</span>
        <span class="blog-meta__dot"></span>
        <span>October 20, 2026</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <article class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="blog-body">

        <div class="blog-intro">
          <p>Valley nights are getting cooler, and this is the time of year pool heaters get put back to work. It's also when we get the most calls about heaters that won't turn on or won't warm the water. A lot of heater problems have a simple cause. Here's what to look for, what you can safely check yourself, and how we handle a pool heater repair.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="blog-section">
          <span class="eyebrow">Warning Signs</span>
          <h2>Signs Your Pool Heater Needs Repair</h2>
          <p>Heater problems usually show up in one of five ways.</p>
          <ul class="blog-check-list">
            <li><span><strong>It won't turn on.</strong> No sound, no flame, nothing. Low water flow, a gas supply problem, or a failed control board are the usual causes.</span></li>
            <li><span><strong>It turns on, then shuts off.</strong> A heater that keeps cycling on and off is often reacting to a flow problem or overheating.</span></li>
            <li><span><strong>It runs but the water stays cold.</strong> A dirty burner, a failing part, or, for heat pumps, cold outside air.</span></li>
            <li><span><strong>It shows an error code.</strong> Most heaters display a code that points to the problem, like a pressure switch or ignition fault.</span></li>
            <li><span><strong>It's leaking or rusting.</strong> Water or heavy corrosion around the heater can mean a damaged heat exchanger.</span></li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <hr class="blog-divider"/>

        <div class="blog-section">
          <span class="eyebrow">Check It Yourself</span>
          <h2>What You Can Safely Check Before You Call</h2>
          <p>These four checks solve more heater problems than you'd think.</p>
          <ul class="blog-check-list">
            <li><span><strong>Thermostat setting.</strong> Make sure the heater is on and the temperature is set higher than the current water temperature.</span></li>
            <li><span><strong>Water flow.</strong> Clean the filter and empty the pump and skimmer baskets. A heater won't fire without enough water moving through it.</span></li>
            <li><span><strong>Valves.</strong> Confirm the valves going to and from the heater are open.</span></li>
            <li><span><strong>Gas supply.</strong> If you have a gas heater, make sure the gas valve is on and other gas appliances in the house are working.</span></li>
          </ul>

          <div class="callout-warn">
            <p>Stop there. If you smell gas, shut off the gas supply and call your gas company right away. Don't open the heater cabinet or work on the burners, igniter, or wiring. Pool heaters combine gas and high voltage, and repairs should be done by a professional.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <hr class="blog-divider"/>

        <div class="blog-section">
          <span class="eyebrow">Our Process</span>
          <h2>How We Handle a Pool Heater Repair</h2>
          <p>Every heater call follows the same steps, so we fix the actual problem instead of guessing.</p>

          <div class="step-list">
            <div class="step-item">
              <div class="step-item__num">1</div>
              <div class="step-item__body">
                <h3>Ask what the heater is doing</h3>
                <p>Silent, cycling, running cold, or showing a code. Each symptom points us to a different part of the system.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="step-item">
              <div class="step-item__num">2</div>
              <div class="step-item__body">
                <h3>Check flow and settings first</h3>
                <p>We check the filter pressure, baskets, valves, and heater settings. Flow problems cause a large share of heater calls, and they're quick to fix.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="step-item">
              <div class="step-item__num">3</div>
              <div class="step-item__body">
                <h3>Read the error code</h3>
                <p>If the heater shows a code, we use it to narrow down the fault before opening anything up.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="step-item">
              <div class="step-item__num">4</div>
              <div class="step-item__body">
                <h3>Test the key parts</h3>
                <p>We test the pressure switch, thermostat, igniter, gas valve, and control board, and we inspect the burners and heat exchanger for damage or debris.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="step-item">
              <div class="step-item__num">5</div>
              <div class="step-item__body">
                <h3>Give you an honest quote</h3>
                <p>We look at the heater's age and condition and tell you plainly whether a repair or a replacement makes more sense.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="step-item">
              <div class="step-item__num">6</div>
              <div class="step-item__body">
                <h3>Repair it and test it</h3>
                <p>We make the repair, run the heater through a full cycle, and confirm it's heating the water before we leave.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <hr class="blog-divider"/>

        <div class="blog-section">
          <span class="eyebrow">Common Fixes</span>
          <h2>The Pool Heater Repairs We Do Most</h2>
          <p><strong>Pressure switch.</strong> This switch keeps the heater from firing without enough water flow. When it fails or gets clogged, the heater won't start.</p>
          <p><strong>Igniter and flame sensor.</strong> These light the burners and confirm the flame. A worn igniter or dirty sensor causes heaters that click but never fire.</p>
          <p><strong>Thermostat and control board.</strong> When the electronics fail, the heater can't read the water temperature or tell the burners when to run.</p>
          <p><strong>Burners.</strong> Spiders, ants, and debris love heater cabinets. Cleaning the burner tray fixes a surprising number of weak or uneven flames.</p>
        </div>

        <hr class="blog-divider"/>

        <div class="blog-section">
          <span class="eyebrow">Repair or Replace</span>
          <h2>When Replacing the Heater Is the Smarter Move</h2>
          <p>Pool heaters are built to last, which is part of why they cost what they do. Lifespan depends a lot on the brand, but it's not unusual for a quality heater to run 15 years or more. An older heater that stops working is often one part or one blockage away from running like new.</p>

          <div class="blog-tip">
            <div class="blog-tip__label">From a Recent Job</div>
            <p>One of our clients has a heater that's 13 years old. It stopped heating, and it kept jumping every time it turned on. When we opened it up, we found a lot of plastic stuck in the pipes, and the plastic had damaged the heater bypass. We replaced the bypass and cleared out all the plastic, and the heater worked as good as new. That was three years ago, and it still runs perfectly.</p>
          </div>

          <p>A cracked heat exchanger or heavy corrosion changes the math. That kind of repair can cost close to a new unit. We'll give you both numbers so you can make the call.</p>
          <p>If you do need a new heater, don't cheap out, especially on a gas heater. A cheap or used gas heater can lead to gas leaks or misfires. When a replacement is the right choice, we install Pentair, Jandy, and Hayward heaters. Those are the only three brands we use.</p>
        </div>

        <hr class="blog-divider"/>

        <div class="blog-section">
          <span class="eyebrow">Next Steps</span>
          <h2>Need Your Pool Heater Looked At?</h2>
          <p>If your pool heater won't turn on or won't heat, give GK Swimming Pools a call. We're a family-run company, three generations in, and we handle pool heater repair throughout the San Fernando Valley and Santa Clarita. We'll find the problem and tell you honestly whether it's worth repairing.</p>

          <div class="blog-cta-inline">
            <a href="/services/pool-repairs" class="btn btn--primary">Pool Repair Services</a>
            <a href="tel:8188048204" class="btn btn--outline">(818) 804-8204</a>
          </div>
        </div>

      </div>
    </div>
  </article>

  <div class="cta-band">
    <div class="container">
      <h2>Pool Heater Giving You Trouble?</h2>
      <p>We'll diagnose it and give you an honest answer on repair versus replacement. Serving the San Fernando Valley and Santa Clarita.</p>
      <a href="/contact" class="btn btn--primary">Get In Touch</a>
    </div>
  </div>

</Layout>

<style>
  .blog-hero { position:relative; height:460px; overflow:hidden; display:flex; align-items:flex-end; }
  .blog-hero__bg { position:absolute; inset:0; }
  .blog-hero__bg img { width:100%; height:100%; object-fit:cover; object-position:center 40%; display:block; }
  .blog-hero__overlay { position:absolute; inset:0; background:linear-gradient(to top, rgba(6,47,97,.90) 0%, rgba(6,47,97,.35) 60%, rgba(6,47,97,.12) 100%); }
  .blog-hero__inner { position:relative; z-index:2; padding-bottom: var(--space-12); }
  .blog-hero__inner .eyebrow { color:var(--color-aqua); margin-bottom: var(--space-3); }
  .blog-hero__inner h1 { color:var(--color-white); font-size:clamp(1.7rem,3.6vw,2.6rem); margin-bottom: var(--space-4); max-width:820px; }
  .blog-meta { display:flex; align-items:center; gap: var(--space-3); color:rgba(255,255,255,.72); font-size:var(--text-sm); font-weight:600; flex-wrap:wrap; }
  .blog-meta__dot { width:4px; height:4px; border-radius:50%; background:var(--color-aqua); }

  .blog-body { max-width:760px; margin:0 auto; }
  .blog-intro { background:var(--color-mist); border-left:4px solid var(--color-aqua); padding:var(--space-6) var(--space-8); margin-bottom:var(--space-12); border-radius:0 var(--radius-sm) var(--radius-sm) 0; }
  .blog-intro p { font-size:var(--text-lg); color:var(--color-deep); font-weight:600; line-height:1.7; margin:0; }
  .blog-section { margin-bottom:var(--space-12); }
  .blog-section .eyebrow { margin-bottom: var(--space-3); }
  .blog-section h2 { margin-bottom: var(--space-6); }
  .blog-section p { color:var(--color-muted); line-height:1.85; margin-bottom: var(--space-4); }
  .blog-section strong { color:var(--color-deep); }
  .blog-check-list { list-style:none; margin: var(--space-4) 0; display:flex; flex-direction:column; gap: var(--space-3); }
  .blog-check-list li { display:flex; align-items:flex-start; gap: var(--space-3); font-size:var(--text-base); color:var(--color-muted); line-height:1.7; }
  .blog-check-list li::before { content:"\2713"; color:var(--color-aqua); font-weight:800; font-size:1rem; flex-shrink:0; margin-top:2px; }
  .blog-divider { border:none; border-top:2px solid var(--color-border); margin:var(--space-12) 0; }

  /* REPAIR STEPS */
  .step-list { display:flex; flex-direction:column; gap:var(--space-3); margin:var(--space-6) 0; }
  .step-item {
    display:flex; gap:var(--space-4); align-items:flex-start;
    background:var(--color-white); border:1.5px solid var(--color-border);
    border-radius:var(--radius-sm); padding:var(--space-4) var(--space-6);
  }
  .step-item__num {
    flex-shrink:0; width:38px; height:38px; border-radius:50%;
    background:var(--color-aqua); color:var(--color-deep);
    font-family:var(--font-heading); font-weight:800; font-size:1.05rem;
    display:flex; align-items:center; justify-content:center; line-height:1;
  }
  .step-item__body h3 {
    font-family:var(--font-heading); font-weight:700; font-size:var(--text-base);
    color:var(--color-deep); text-transform:uppercase; letter-spacing:.03em;
    margin-bottom:var(--space-2);
  }
  .step-item__body p { font-size:var(--text-sm); color:var(--color-muted); line-height:1.7; margin:0; }

  /* WARNING CALLOUT */
  .callout-warn {
    background:var(--color-white); border:1.5px solid var(--color-border);
    border-left:3px solid #e8a33d; border-radius:var(--radius-sm);
    padding:var(--space-6); margin:var(--space-6) 0;
  }
  .callout-warn p { font-size:var(--text-sm); color:var(--color-muted); line-height:1.8; margin:0; }

  .blog-tip { background:var(--color-deep); border-radius:var(--radius-sm); padding: var(--space-6) var(--space-8); margin: var(--space-8) 0; }
  .blog-tip__label { font-size:var(--text-xs); font-weight:700; letter-spacing:.15em; text-transform:uppercase; color:var(--color-aqua); margin-bottom: var(--space-2); font-family:var(--font-heading); }
  .blog-tip p { color:rgba(255,255,255,.88); margin:0; }

  .blog-cta-inline { display:flex; gap: var(--space-4); flex-wrap:wrap; margin-top: var(--space-8); }
  .btn--outline { display:inline-block; border:2px solid var(--color-deep); color:var(--color-deep); font-family:var(--font-heading); font-weight:700; font-size:var(--text-sm); letter-spacing:.06em; text-transform:uppercase; padding: var(--space-4) var(--space-8); border-radius:var(--radius-sm); text-decoration:none; }

  @media (max-width:640px) {
    .blog-hero { height:360px; }
    .blog-intro { padding: var(--space-4); }
    .step-item { gap:var(--space-4); padding:var(--space-4); }
    .blog-cta-inline { flex-direction:column; }
  }
</style>
